==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|la|en}} ''[[nil#Latin|nīl]]'' ‘nothing’, ''[[desperandum|dēspērandum]]'', gerund of ''[[desperare|dēspērāre]]'' ‘despair’, as used in ''Nil desperandum Teucro duce et auspice Teucro'' ‘no need to despair with Teucer as your leader and Teucer to protect you’, in [[w:Horace|Horace]], ''Odes''

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/nɪl dɛspəˈɹændəm/}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# Do not [[despair]].
#* '''1747''', Rapin de Thoyras, ''The History of England, Volume XXII'', James and John Knapton, page 408:
#*: It is now, Holy Father, your business to accompany him by 1707-8. colours and standards were, ''Dieu & mon Droit'', "God and my Right:" '''''Nil desperandum''' Christo duce & auspice Christo'', "I ought not to despair, since Christ is my guide and helper:

